# Credits and acknowledgements

This project is MIT-licensed, authored by Sven Hinrichs (see LICENSE). It
contains no code from any of the tools below; they are listed because the work
would not have taken this shape without them. The distinction that matters for
a license audit: **prior art** was read to understand algorithms and nothing was
copied; **bundled models** ship in `models/` under their own licences;
**dependencies** are installed separately by the user.

## Prior art (studied, no code taken)

* **chsasank/Image-Rectification** (MIT) — the starting point of this work.
  Implements Chaudhury et al. (ICIP 2014); run and measured before being
  rewritten. See `docs/reference-review.md`.
* **darktable's `ashift` module** (GPL-3.0) — studied for algorithm design only.
  The 28 mm default, the "reject a vanishing point inside the frame" rule, the
  ~4x warp-area refusal and the scale-free RANSAC residual all trace back to it.
  No GPL code is included or derived from. See `docs/prior-art.md`.
* **ShiftN** (Marcus Hebel, LGPL) — the ancestor of the photographic line:
  correct the verticals, damp the correction, let the user override. Its Burns
  detector is the ancestor of LSD. Nothing copied.
* **Lee et al., "Automatic Upright Adjustment of Photographs" (TPAMI 2014)** —
  the model here follows this shape: one joint estimate of (world-up, focal
  length), not two independent detections.

## Bundled models (`models/`)

These files are redistributed with the project and keep their own licences.

* **`M-LSD_512_large_fp32.tflite`** — Apache-2.0, full text in
  `models/LICENSE.mlsd`. Gu et al., "M-LSD: A Wireframe Model for Line Segment
  Detection" (AAAI 2022). Used by `--detector mlsd`, `hybrid` and `union`; needs
  a TFLite runtime (`ai-edge-litert`).
* **`deeplsd_md.tar`** — MIT. Pautrat et al., "DeepLSD: Deep Learning Based Line
  Segment Detector" (CVPR 2023), the MegaDepth model from `cvg/DeepLSD`. Used by
  `--detector deeplsd`, `deep-hybrid` and `deep-union`; needs torch and a checkout.

## Dependencies (`requirements.txt`)

Installed separately; none are vendored.

* **numpy** — BSD-3-Clause.
* **OpenCV** (`opencv-python` / `opencv-python-headless`) — Apache-2.0 (v4).
  Provides the LSD line detector (Morel & Devy, "LSD: Line Segment Detector",
  ICCV 2009), restored in OpenCV 4.8 after being dropped in 4.1–4.7.
* **Pillow** — HPND.
* **piexif** — MIT. Reads EXIF orientation and focal length.

## Optional backends (user-installed, not bundled)

Each is imported lazily and says what is missing instead of failing at import.

* **BiRefNet** — a third-party salient-object segmentation model (torch). Weights
  are supplied separately (`--birefnet-model`); `--mask-export` bridges the
  interpreter split.
* **LaMa** (`simple-lama-inpainting`, MIT) — inpainting for `--fill lama`.
  Install with `--no-deps`: a plain install downgrades Pillow and numpy and
  breaks OpenCV in the same interpreter.
* **ComfyUI** — outpaint backend for `--fill comfyui`; the workflow is a file in
  `workflows/`.

## Algorithms implemented classically, after their papers

The geometry is standard camera calibration; no learned prior is used on the
default path. Where a method was read rather than reimplemented, it is cited in
`docs/prior-art.md`: Rother (2002), Tardif / J-Linkage (2009), Bazin &
Pollefeys (2012), Chaudhury et al. (2014), Lezama et al. (2014), Zhai et al.
(2016), NeurVPS (2019), VaPiD (2021) and GeoCalib (2024).
